# Flow Life Commons: the production document root (docs/architecture/deployment-topology.md).
#
# This file governs the WHOLE production origin, not just Laravel: the Guardian Console's index.html
# and hashed assets are served straight from here by Apache and never reach PHP, so both the browser
# security policy (ADR 0026) and the routing contract (ADR 0027) have to be stated at this level.
#
# ORDER IS THE DESIGN. The sections below run in the order they are written, and each one exists
# because an earlier or later position would be wrong:
#
#   1. directory behaviour   no listings, no content negotiation, and / resolves to the Console
#   2. security headers      GENERATED; `Header always set` applies to every response, this file's
#                            403s and the maintenance 503 included
#   3. private paths         FIRST of the rewrites, so no later rule — maintenance or SPA fallback —
#                            can turn a request for a private path into a 200 or a 503 page
#   4. maintenance           before routing, so an outage is answered before anything else is
#                            considered; excludes /api, /up and this responder itself
#   5. Laravel's own rules   header forwarding and the trailing-slash redirect, unchanged
#   6. API and /up           explicit, so an API path can never fall through to the Console shell;
#                            [END] so Apache's internal redirect to index.php does not re-run this
#                            ruleset and let section 4 catch the rewritten request on a second pass
#   7. SPA fallback          LAST, and only for what is neither a real file nor a real directory
#
# The composed contract is exercised in a browser against the production-equivalent development
# origin (apps/guardian-console/e2e/production-surface.spec.ts) and pinned structurally by
# tests/Feature/Modules/Security/ProductionSurfaceTest.php. Rule ordering is where a file like this
# goes wrong, so both tests assert order, not just presence.

# --- 1. Directory behaviour -------------------------------------------------------------------
# DirectoryIndex puts the Console's shell ahead of the front controller, so `/` is the Console and
# not Laravel (deployment topology). -Indexes because a directory listing on a document root is a
# map of the deployment; -MultiViews because content negotiation can serve `/index` as `index.php`.
<IfModule mod_negotiation.c>
    Options -MultiViews
</IfModule>
Options -Indexes
DirectoryIndex index.html index.php

# --- 2. Browser security policy ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# GENERATED from apps/platform/config/security.php by `php artisan security:headers --format=apache`.
# Do not hand-edit it: a test compares this file with that command's output, so an edit here that is
# not in the config fails the build (and vice versa).
#
# It needs mod_headers. If the hosting account does not have it, the Console's static files ship with
# NO security headers while the API keeps them, which is a materially weaker origin — verified
# present on this host on 2026-09-21 (production readiness, item R7).
#
# EACH header is `onsuccess unset` before it is `always set`. Laravel's own middleware
# (ApplyBrowserSecurityHeaders) sets these same seven headers on every response it answers, so without
# the unset, Apache's `always set` APPENDS a second copy instead of replacing PHP's: measured on a real
# Apache 2.4 container serving this exact file, every response the front controller answered — 200,
# 301, 302, 400, 403, 404, 500 and 503 alike — carried each header twice. The unset clears whatever PHP
# sent regardless of that response's status, so `always set`'s value is always the one a client
# receives, and it is sent exactly once whether Apache or Laravel answered the request.

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
    Header onsuccess unset Content-Security-Policy
    Header always set Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'none'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
    Header onsuccess unset X-Content-Type-Options
    Header always set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
    Header onsuccess unset Referrer-Policy
    Header always set Referrer-Policy "same-origin"
    Header onsuccess unset X-Frame-Options
    Header always set X-Frame-Options "DENY"
    Header onsuccess unset Permissions-Policy
    Header always set Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=(), display-capture=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=()"
    Header onsuccess unset Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
    Header always set Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy "same-origin"
    Header onsuccess unset Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy
    Header always set Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy "same-origin"
    Header onsuccess unset Strict-Transport-Security
    Header always set Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000" "expr=%{HTTPS} == 'on'"
</IfModule>

<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
    RewriteEngine On

    # --- 3. Private paths, denied before anything else ----------------------------------------
    #
    # Defence in depth, not the primary control: the document root is the release's `public/`, so
    # `.env`, `vendor/`, `storage/` and the source are physically outside the served tree already
    # (ADR 0027). These rules are what answers if that is ever untrue — a mis-set document root, a
    # stray copy, an unpacked archive — and they come first so that neither the maintenance arm nor
    # the SPA fallback can answer for a private path instead.
    #
    # Any path segment beginning with a dot: `/.env`, and `/.git/config`, whose FILE name does not
    # begin with a dot and which a <FilesMatch "^\."> would therefore miss. `.well-known` is the one
    # exception, because cPanel's certificate renewal serves ACME challenges from it.
    RewriteRule "(^|/)\.(?!well-known/)" - [F,L]

    # Application directories, if one ever lands inside the document root.
    RewriteRule "^(app|bootstrap|config|database|routes|storage|tests|node_modules|vendor)(/|$)" - [F,L]

    # Application files, likewise. release.json names the commit and is not the public's business
    # (ADR 0027: release identity stays off the public surface).
    RewriteRule "^(artisan|composer\.(json|lock)|package(-lock)?\.json|release\.json|phpunit\.xml|phpstan\.neon)$" - [F,L]

    # --- 4. Maintenance -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    #
    # One authority, two enforcement points (ADR 0027). The flag is Laravel's own
    # `storage/framework/down`, reached from this document root through the shared storage symlink;
    # the `-f` test was verified against the real host on 2026-09-21, symlink and all.
    #
    # An INTERNAL rewrite with [L]. Never `R=503`, and never `ErrorDocument`: that idiom was tried on
    # this host and returned the server's own bare 503 body instead of the page. The status, and the
    # headers that go with it, come from maintenance.php itself.
    #
    # The exclusions are the API carve-out. `/api` is excluded with and without a trailing path, so a
    # bare `/api` still reaches Laravel and is answered as JSON: a static HTML 503 delivered to an API
    # client is worse than the outage it reports. `/up` is Laravel's liveness probe and answers 503
    # itself while down. maintenance.php excludes itself, or the internal rewrite would loop.
    RewriteCond %{DOCUMENT_ROOT}/../storage/framework/down -f
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} !^/(api($|/)|up$|maintenance\.php$)
    RewriteRule ^ /maintenance.php [L]

    # --- 5. Laravel's own rules, unchanged ----------------------------------------------------

    # Handle Authorization Header
    RewriteCond %{HTTP:Authorization} .
    RewriteRule .* - [E=HTTP_AUTHORIZATION:%{HTTP:Authorization}]

    # Handle X-XSRF-Token Header
    RewriteCond %{HTTP:x-xsrf-token} .
    RewriteRule .* - [E=HTTP_X_XSRF_TOKEN:%{HTTP:X-XSRF-Token}]

    # Redirect Trailing Slashes If Not A Folder...
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_URI} (.+)/$
    RewriteRule ^ %1 [L,R=301]

    # --- 6. The API and the liveness probe belong to Laravel ----------------------------------
    #
    # Explicit, and before the fallback: without this an unknown API path would be neither a file nor
    # a directory, fall through to section 7 and be answered with the Console's HTML shell and a 200.
    # Laravel must answer it, with its own JSON 404. This is the same split the development gateway
    # makes (`path /api /api/* /up`), and the reason no Console route may begin with /api or be /up.
    #
    # [END], not [L]. `.htaccess` is per-directory context: `[L]` ends only the CURRENT pass, and a
    # rewrite to a real file (index.php) triggers Apache's own internal redirect, which re-runs this
    # whole ruleset from the top as a SECOND pass. On that second pass %{REQUEST_URI} is /index.php,
    # which the section 4 exclusion does not name, so the maintenance rule matched it and sent every
    # /api and /up request to the HTML responder instead of Laravel — measured on a real Apache 2.4
    # container with this file while the flag was raised. `[END]` stops mod_rewrite processing for the
    # request outright, so no second pass happens and section 4 is never re-evaluated. Requires Apache
    # 2.4.24+ (mod_rewrite), which every CloudLinux build in current use carries; verified locally on
    # 2.4.68 and unverified beyond that on the production host.
    RewriteRule "^(api($|/)|up$)" index.php [END]

    # --- 7. The Console, and its client-side routes -------------------------------------------
    #
    # LAST. A real file or directory is served as-is — index.html, the hashed assets, favicon.ico,
    # robots.txt — and everything else is the Console's shell, so a deep link like /people/123 boots
    # the Console instead of returning 404 (deployment topology).
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
    RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
    RewriteRule ^ /index.html [L]
</IfModule>
